{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"But they were covered with gold leaf, which had engraved stories on it, of which some pieces are still preserved. On this throne, 300 years after his death, Karl was found sitting, I believe, by Emperor Friedrich, with the imperial regalia put on him, the crown on his head, the scepter in one hand, and the orb in the other; these items were added to the Imperial Regalia and his bones were interred. I sat on this throne, on which 32 emperors had been crowned, as the sacristan assured me, just as well as anyone else, and the whole satisfaction is that one has sat on it. — But the main thing was that I saw the collection of Mr. Bettendorf (which is now being sold individually) for 3 hours in the morning and likewise in the afternoon, and he had the kindness to keep me company himself. In regard to Old German art, it is the counterpart to the Boisseree collection; both combined, as both gentlemen intended — purchased by the King — would represent the most magnificent thing in this sphere of art. Mr. Bettendorf does not have such large and distinguished van Eycks as Boisseree, but his Hemlings, one picture in particular, are at least as excellent as the Boisseree ones. Some of the figures in the Boisseree Hemlings, especially the Jew gathering manna, is exactly the same one who cuts the Paschal lamb on the Bettendorf picture. — But one picture by a Roger is the highest thing one can see; every particularity — some dryness that one would wish to remove even from the most excellent Eyck works — has completely disappeared here; it is as magnificently Italian as it is Netherlandish. A jewel is likewise a picture — a Deposition from the Cross with many figures, drawn by Raphael and painted by Albrecht Dürer, what loveliness, what beauty!! — A woman with a child — attributed by some to Michelangelo — is an infinitely great painting. But above all, a night by Correggio! — just as I called the Dresden one the day of Correggio, so this is the true night. What a picture! The light emanating equally from the child — Mary is dearer to me here than in the Dresden one, and she, like the surroundings in this one, smiles — everything is also cheerful — but more serious, and the darkness, as in the Correggio pictures in Sanssouci, of the later manner of this master — of the highest excellence. In the evening I still took a walk towards Burtscheid, and since the famous bath is in Aachen, I took a bath: it is hot! and full of sulphur smell. — On Tuesday morning at 7 o'clock we left Aachen and arrived in Liège around 5 o'clock; the road goes up hill, down hill, mostly on a ridge, with deeper valleys on both sides, everything green, intersected by innumerable hedges and rows of trees. Towards Liège one gets views into the beautiful Meuse valley; I was very tempted to travel from Liège up the Meuse valley via Namur to here — but I would have spent nearly two days longer, because of the curse of the carriages, and part of the journey would have been made at night, when no one sees anything. In Liège I stayed overnight with one of the travelers; the carriage on which we had arrived went straight on; among the traveling party there was just again such a flat, chatty German — a Würzburger, who also wants to be an Englishman — annoying people, as I had had one in every carriage party so far. My"}}],"created":1795080284,"model":"qwen","system_fingerprint":"b11429-d81235049","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":770,"prompt_tokens":938,"total_tokens":1708,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-eiSsTjIjq4hCyT8cmluty0QxzCe8gTcN","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":938,"prompt_ms":991.236,"prompt_per_token_ms":1.0567547974413647,"prompt_per_second":946.2933146092354,"predicted_n":770,"predicted_ms":18419.008,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.95189596879064,"predicted_per_second":41.75034833580614}}